'''Patrick Sammie Mills''' {{post-nominals|country=AUS|AM}} (born 11 August 1988) is an Australian professional [[basketball]] player who last played for [[CB Canarias|La Laguna Tenerife]] of the [[Liga ACB]]. He is the general manager of the [[Hawaii Rainbow Warriors basketball]] program at the [[University of Hawai'i at Mānoa]]. Mills was born and raised in [[Canberra]], and is of [[Torres Strait Islanders|Torres Strait Islander]] and [[Aboriginal Australian]] ([[Kokatha]] and [[Meriam people|Dauareb-Meriam]]<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2021/jul/08/patty-mills-cate-campbell-named-australias-olympic-flagbearers|title=Patty Mills, Cate Campbell named Australia's Olympic flagbearers|work=The Guardian|date=8 July 2021|quote=“As a proud Kokatha, Naghiralgal and Dauareb-Meriam man it’s incredible,” Mills said from Las Vegas.}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/olympics/57746540|title=Tokyo 2020: Patty Mills to be first Indigenous Australian to carry flag at Games|work=BBC News|date=7 July 2021|quote="As a proud Kokatha, Naghiralgal and Dauareb-Meriam man it's incredible," he continued.}}</ref>) descent. In 2007, he became the third Indigenous basketball player to play for the [[Australia men's national basketball team|Australian national team]]. Mills was selected by the [[Portland Trail Blazers]] with the 55th overall pick in the [[2009 NBA draft]] after playing two years of [[college basketball]] for the [[Saint Mary's Gaels men's basketball|Saint Mary's Gaels]].

Mills played for the Portland Trail Blazers for two seasons. In 2011, during the [[2011 NBA lockout|NBA lockout]], he played for the [[Melbourne United|Melbourne Tigers]] of the [[National Basketball League (Australia)|National Basketball League]] (NBL) and for the [[Xinjiang Flying Tigers]] in China. Mills returned to the United States in March 2012 and signed with the [[San Antonio Spurs]]. He became a strong contributor off the bench and helped the Spurs win the [[2014 NBA Finals|2014 NBA championship]]. In 2021 he led the [[Australian Boomers]] to their first-ever Olympic medal at the [[Tokyo 2020 Olympics]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Healy |first=Jon |date=7 August 2021 |title=After finishing fourth four times, the Boomers finally get on the Olympic podium |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2021-08-07/tokyo-olympics-patty-mills-leads-boomers-to-bronze-medal/100359056 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211123180000/https://www.abc.net.au/news/2021-08-07/tokyo-olympics-patty-mills-leads-boomers-to-bronze-medal/100359056 |archive-date=23 November 2021 |access-date=25 November 2021 |website=ABC.net.au}}</ref> While with the Boomers, he is often referred to as "FIBA Patty" for his high level performances in the Olympics where he is the 5th highest scorer in Olympic basketball history.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-08-06 |title=Just short: Clutch vintage FIBA Patty can't get Olympics revenge |url=https://www.fiba.basketball/en/events/mens-olympic-basketball-tournament-paris-2024/news/just-short-clutch-vintage-fiba-patty-cant-get-olympics-revenge |access-date=2024-10-25 |website=fiba.basketball |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-08-06 |title=Once again, 'FIBA Patty' put on a show at the Olympics. But it wasn't enough for Australia this time |url=https://apnews.com/article/olympics-2024-patty-mills-australia-a6cafcef663bf8c8eca1b523f8e84e07 |access-date=2024-10-25 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-08-02 |title=Mills jumps to No. 5 all-time in scoring despite Boomers loss |url=https://www.fiba.basketball/en/events/mens-olympic-basketball-tournament-paris-2024/news/mills-jumps-to-no-5-all-time-in-scoring-despite-boomers-loss |access-date=2024-10-25 |website=fiba.basketball |language=en}}</ref> Mills is well known for his three-point shooting and his leadership qualities.

